  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to use a sparse sampling technique combined with a convex optimization formulation. The Erdos minimum overlap problem can be restructured as a constrained optimization with sparsity promoting regularization. This differs from the original method by not fixing the number of sample points but rather allowing them to adjust dynamically. The key limitations are:\n   - Mathematical justification: Formulate the problem with explicit sparsity constraints and convex smoothness terms\n   - Implementation distinction: Solve using a DC (difference of convex functions) programming framework rather than traditional local search\n   - Rationale: Sparsity can model piecewise constant features efficiently while maintaining global smoothness. This approach should explore finely varying solutions that better adapt to shift minima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresent h:R\u21a6[0,1] as a piecewise constant function on a grid with n samples, but allow n to be dynamically adjusted. The objective becomes:\n   $$\\minimize_{h} \\max_{k} \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx$$\n   subject to:\n   $$\\int_0^2 h(x)dx = 1 \\quad \\forall x, h(x) \\in [0,1]$$\n\nThe restructured formulation:\n$$\\begin{array}{ll} \\minimize & t \\\\ \\text{subject to} & t \\geq \\langle h \\star (1-h), c \\rangle \\\\ & \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i h_i = \\frac{n}{2} dx \\\\ & |h_i| \\leq 1 \\\\ & \\|h\\|_{p} \\leq s \\end{array}$$\nwhere h is transformed to sparse representation with sparsity s. The inner product is taken with a filter c defined by the optimal kernel.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Population initialization\n   Use the parent solution as base, but generate multiple variants through:\n   - Random switching between sparse and dense representations\n   - Introducing deliberate violations then applying projection\n   - Frequency domain manipulation: preserving spectrum while altering sparsity\n\nb) Core update mechanism\n   Apply gradient descent for the max-constraint using:\n   $$\\nabla_{h} \\langle h \\star (1-h), c \\rangle = (h \\star \\text{diag}(1-h)) \\text{filter}$$\n   with filter from discrete Fourier transform.\n\nc) Guidance vector construction\n   Build a decision tree from previous successful searches:\n   - Positive/negative regions identified from correlation profiles\n   - Intervals where solutions have been seen to improve\n   - Difference vectors from top to bottom performers\n\nd) Projection strategy\n   Implement adaptive projection:\n   - For integral: normalize by median value selection\n   - For bounds: mix of thresholding and slacks\n   - For sparsity: use thresholding $\\|h\\|_p \\leq s$\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nInitialization strategies:\n- Adaptive mcintoch initialization: simulate topological structure from (0.38,0.39) bound solutions\n- Multi-start: 3 distinct variants per initial population\n- Dynamic sparsity: initially low sparsity, then stabilize\n\nIteration processes:\n- Primary search: 100 iterations with adaptive alpha (3-5)\n- Validation passes: every 5 iterations restart slows with radius \n- Exploration burst: 25% of iterations use direction vectors from boundaries\n\nDivergence handling:\n- Noise threshold: when correlation drops below 0.30s\n- Mixing ratio update: count successes\n- Endurance check: terminate if stuck for overlap max below 0.39 for 5 cycles\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nWe estimate 100 seconds for 10,000 evaluations:\n- Sample points: 100-200 for initial constraint satisfaction\n- Full resolution: 200-400 samples\n- Sparsity levels: 10-50 active coefficients\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Gradient clipping: limit slopes to \u00b10.5 of initial\n- Feasibility checkpoints: every 100 function evaluations\n- Budget overflow: dynamically increase complexity if plateauing\n- Resource lock: use seeding for entropic decoding\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nCandidates must satisfy:\n- All hticks between 0 and 1\n- Integral exactly 0.5\n- fmin bound below 0.385\n\nVerification procedures:\n1. Check filter is active for all lags\n2. Verify inversion under correlation\n3. Compute dc metrics using entanglement condition\n\nFallback solution:\n- When stuck, use projected frank-wolfe with entropy\n- Format: (h_samples, bound, n_points)\n- Retain parent as test oracle\n\nThe fallback solution is pre-signed and available via the erdos.environment.fallback_envelope for direct invocation.",
